French law has no offence of hitchhiking; what it restricts is being a pedestrian on the fast roads. [https://www.legifrance.gouv.fr/codes/article_lc/LEGIARTI000006842313 Article R421-2 of the ''code de la route''] bars pedestrians — along with animals, cycles and mopeds — from the ''autoroute'', and that covers the hard shoulder, the slip roads and the toll plaza, not only the carriageway. Drivers cannot legally stop for you on it either: [https://www.legifrance.gouv.fr/codes/article_lc/LEGIARTI000006842319 article R421-7] forbids stopping or parking on an autoroute's carriageways and verges, ''bandes d'arrêt d'urgence'' included, except in absolute necessity. The lawful places are therefore the on-ramp before the blue ''autoroute'' sign, and the ''aires de service'' and ''aires de repos'', where you ask drivers face to face rather than thumb. On ''routes nationales'' and ''départementales'' hitchhiking is unregulated and legal; [https://www.legifrance.gouv.fr/codes/section_lc/LEGITEXT000006074228/LEGISCTA000006177125/ article R412-36] tells pedestrians outside built-up areas to keep to the left edge of the road, facing the traffic.
